---
// Example article page. Everything here is sample copy and placeholder images, written
// to judge the Article pattern in CLAUDE.md. Replace or delete it once the content model
// exists, the same as dev-check.astro.
//
// The index groups are parts of this one article and the entries are its sections, the way
// the Cargo reference's index groups the sections of a single long page. CLAUDE.md's
// working assumption is groups = kits and entries = articles, which waits on the content
// model.
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import ArticleIndex from "../components/ArticleIndex.astro";
import ArticleSection from "../components/ArticleSection.astro";
import ArticleFigure from "../components/ArticleFigure.astro";
import ArticleGallery from "../components/ArticleGallery.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import NewsRows from "../components/NewsRows.astro";
import Lightbox from "../components/Lightbox.astro";
import { keep, byDateDesc, formatDate, isoDate } from "../lib/content";

const date = new Date("2026-11-14");

// This page is reached from a news entry whose url is its path. Related news is the other
// entries: the same topic first, then the rest, newest first within each. A page with no
// entry of its own (the production build drops the samples) gets every entry, or none.
const path = "/article-example/";
const allNews = (await getCollection("news", keep)).sort(byDateDesc);
const self = allNews.find((e) => e.data.url === path);
const others = allNews.filter((e) => e !== self);
const related = [
	...others.filter((e) => self?.data.topic && e.data.topic === self.data.topic),
	...others.filter((e) => !self?.data.topic || e.data.topic !== self.data.topic),
].slice(0, 3);

const groups = [
	{
		name: "The problem",
		items: [
			{ id: "making", title: "Making got cheap" },
			{ id: "checking", title: "Checking did not" },
		],
	},
	{
		name: "The routine",
		items: [
			{ id: "cold", title: "Read it cold" },
			{ id: "brief", title: "Compare with the brief" },
			{ id: "mark", title: "Mark what you would not ship" },
		],
	},
	{
		name: "Afterwards",
		items: [{ id: "rejects", title: "Keep the rejects" }],
	},
];

const nav = [{ href: "/", label: "Home" }];
---

<Base title="Read the output twice | Love&Hate AI">
	<SiteHeader links={nav} rule="right" />

	<main id="main">
		<div class="grid article">
			<div class="side">
				<div class="head">
					<h1>Read the output twice</h1>
					<p class="meta muted tnum">
						<time datetime={isoDate(date)}>{formatDate(date)}</time>
						<span>6 min read</span>
					</p>
				</div>

				<ArticleIndex groups={groups} />
			</div>

			<div class="text">
				<ArticleSection id="making" title="Making got cheap">
					<p>
						A first draft used to take an afternoon. Now it takes the time to type a sentence, and you get a dozen of them
						back. The drafts are not the problem. The problem is that a dozen plausible drafts are harder to judge than
						one rough sketch, because every one of them looks finished.
					</p>
					<p>
						A sketch shows you what it is missing. A generated layout hides what it is missing behind clean kerning and a
						believable grid.
					</p>
				</ArticleSection>

				<ArticleSection id="checking" title="Checking did not">
					<p>
						Nobody got faster at looking. If anything we got slower, because there is more to look at and less reason to
						trust any of it. The making shrank, the review grew, and the review is now the bigger job.
					</p>
					<p>
						This piece is about the review. It assumes you will keep using the tools and want to get better at the half of
						the work they did not remove.
					</p>
				</ArticleSection>

				<ArticleSection id="cold" title="Read it cold">
					<p>
						Open the output without the brief next to it. Say what it is for, out loud, in one sentence. If you cannot,
						either the output is wrong or the brief was, and you found out in thirty seconds.
					</p>
					<h3>Read the small text first</h3>
					<p>
						Labels, legal lines, button copy and dates are where generated work breaks. Nobody prompts for them, so nobody
						checks them, and the model fills them with something that sounds right.
					</p>
					<h3>Count things</h3>
					<p>
						Five nav items where the brief asked for four. Two logos that are almost the same logo. Counting is dull and it
						catches more than taste does.
					</p>
				</ArticleSection>

				<ArticleSection id="brief" title="Compare with the brief">
					<p>
						Put the brief and the output side by side and write the comparison down. Doing it in your head only checks the
						parts you remember asking for.
					</p>
					<ul>
						<li>Each requirement from the brief, one line each</li>
						<li>What the output added that nobody asked for</li>
						<li>What it dropped without saying so</li>
						<li>What you would have to ask someone else about before it could ship</li>
					</ul>
					<p>
						The middle two lines are where the work is. Additions feel like gifts and omissions are invisible, so both slip
						through.
					</p>
					<ArticleFigure
						number={1}
						ratio={[16, 10]}
						caption="The brief on the left, the output on the right, the checklist between them."
					/>
				</ArticleSection>

				<ArticleSection id="mark" title="Mark what you would not ship">
					<p>
						Take a screenshot or print it, and mark it up with a pen or a red layer. Marking forces a decision on each
						element. <em>It's fine</em> is not a decision.
					</p>
					<ArticleGallery
						number={2}
						ratio={[16, 10]}
						slides={[
							{ caption: "Round one, marked up." },
							{ caption: "Round two. The header is fixed and the footer got worse." },
							{ caption: "Round three, with most of the marks gone." },
							{ caption: "What shipped." },
						]}
					/>
					<p>
						Stop after three rounds. If round four is still wrong, the brief is the problem, so rewrite that instead of
						the output.
					</p>
				</ArticleSection>

				<ArticleSection id="rejects" title="Keep the rejects">
					<p>
						Save what you threw away, with one line on why. After a few weeks the pile tells you what your model does wrong
						by default, which is more use than any prompt guide. It also settles arguments, because "we tried that" is
						easy to say and hard to prove.
					</p>
					<p>
						<em>
							None of this is an argument against the tools. It is an argument for doing the dull half of the job on
							purpose.
						</em>
					</p>
				</ArticleSection>
			</div>
		</div>

		{
			related.length > 0 && (
				<Section id="related" title="Related news">
					<NewsRows entries={related} />
				</Section>
			)
		}
	</main>

	<SiteFooter links={nav} />
	<Lightbox />
</Base>

<script>
	// Scroll position in the index, and the gallery. Both are enhancements: without
	// this script the index links still jump and the gallery shows every figure.
	const sections = [...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>("[data-section]")];
	const indexes = [...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>("[data-index]")];
	const readouts = [...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>("[data-pos]")];
	const pad = (n: number) => String(n).padStart(2, "0");

	let current = -1;

	function mark(i: number) {
		if (i === current) return;
		current = i;
		const id = sections[i].id;
		for (const nav of indexes) {
			for (const a of nav.querySelectorAll<HTMLAnchorElement>("a[href^='#']")) {
				if (a.hash === `#${id}`) a.setAttribute("aria-current", "location");
				else a.removeAttribute("aria-current");
			}
		}
		for (const el of readouts) el.textContent = `${pad(i + 1)} / ${pad(sections.length)}`;
	}

	// The current section is the last one whose top has reached the line where a jump
	// lands it (its scroll-margin-top). Level with that line, the index entry and the
	// heading agree. At the very bottom the last section wins, whatever its height.
	function update() {
		const root = document.documentElement;
		const atBottom = window.scrollY + window.innerHeight >= root.scrollHeight - 2;
		let found = 0;
		sections.forEach((s, i) => {
			const line = parseFloat(getComputedStyle(s).scrollMarginTop) || 0;
			if (s.getBoundingClientRect().top <= line + 2) found = i;
		});
		mark(atBottom ? sections.length - 1 : found);
	}

	let queued = false;
	function onScroll() {
		if (queued) return;
		queued = true;
		requestAnimationFrame(() => {
			queued = false;
			update();
		});
	}

	if (sections.length) {
		addEventListener("scroll", onScroll, { passive: true });
		addEventListener("resize", onScroll);
		update();
	}

	// On phones the index is a <details> above the text. Close it after a jump so the
	// text isn't left under an open list.
	for (const d of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLDetailsElement>("details")) {
		d.addEventListener("click", (e) => {
			if ((e.target as HTMLElement).closest("a")) d.open = false;
		});
	}

	// Gallery. One slide shows at a time. The slides are already in the page and share
	// one cell, so swapping only changes visibility.
	for (const gallery of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>("[data-gallery]")) {
		const slides = [...gallery.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>("[data-slide]")];
		const thumbs = [...gallery.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-thumb]")];
		const strip = gallery.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-thumbs]");
		if (!slides.length || !strip) continue;

		const show = (i: number) => {
			slides.forEach((s, j) => s.toggleAttribute("data-current", i === j));
			thumbs.forEach((t, j) => {
				if (i === j) t.setAttribute("aria-current", "true");
				else t.removeAttribute("aria-current");
			});
		};

		thumbs.forEach((t, i) => t.addEventListener("click", () => show(i)));
		gallery.setAttribute("data-ready", "");
		strip.hidden = false;
		show(0);
	}
</script>

<style>
	/* Phones: a plain block, so the sticky "On this page" bar can travel the whole
	   article. A sticky grid item can't leave its own grid row. */
	.article {
		display: block;
	}

	/* The title and the index share one wrapper on wide screens only. Below that it
	   disappears, so its children sit directly in the article, as they did before. */
	.side {
		display: contents;
	}

	.head {
		padding-top: calc(var(--line) * 3);
		padding-bottom: calc(var(--line) * 2);
	}

	.meta {
		margin-top: var(--line);
	}

	.meta > :global(*) {
		display: block;
	}

	/* The related news section brings its own space above it. */
	.article {
		padding-bottom: 0;
	}

	@media (min-width: 48rem) {
		.article {
			display: grid;
		}

		/* Medium: the title across the top, then the index in columns 1-3 (column 1 of
		   four) and the text in 4-12. The measure stays near 80 characters. */
		.head {
			grid-column: 1 / -1;
			grid-row: 1;
			padding-top: calc(var(--line) * 4);
			padding-bottom: calc(var(--line) * 1.5);
		}

		.text {
			grid-column: 4 / -1;
			grid-row: 2;
			max-width: 35rem;
		}
	}

	/* Wide: the title and date, then the index under them, fill the left half and the
	   index sticks as the text scrolls. Columns 7-12 are all text. The title is the
	   page's one experimental move: it sits in the left half and lines up with the first
	   section heading. Prose keeps its 80 character measure, and figures use the full
	   width of the column. */
	@media (min-width: 64rem) {
		.side {
			display: block;
			grid-column: 1 / span 6;
			grid-row: 1;
		}

		/* The index brings its own 1.5 lines above its first line, which is what keeps it
		   level with a section heading once it sticks. So the title block adds none. */
		.head {
			padding-top: calc(var(--line) * 1.5);
			padding-bottom: 0;
		}

		.text {
			grid-column: 7 / -1;
			grid-row: 1;
			max-width: none;
		}
	}
</style>
